A cylindrical coordinate system is a three dimensional coordinate system that specifies point positions around a main axis and an auxiliary axis. 
The three cylindrical coordinates are: 

		1) The point perpendicular distance p from the main axis

		2) The point signed distance z along the main axis from a chosen origin

		3) The plane angle φ of the point projection on a reference plane (passing through the origin and perpendicular to the main axis)
